WEBVTT

00:00.620 --> 00:02.320
It's late at night,
and some type

00:02.320 --> 00:05.860
of nocturnal predatory
bird, maybe this is an owl,

00:05.860 --> 00:08.390
is diving for its dinner.

00:08.390 --> 00:13.500
So this right over
here is a mouse.

00:13.500 --> 00:18.360
And it's diving straight
down near a street light.

00:18.360 --> 00:20.720
And let's get some information
about what's going on.

00:20.720 --> 00:25.610
So the street light right
over here is 20 feet high.

00:25.610 --> 00:31.140
So this is a 20 foot
high street lamp.

00:31.140 --> 00:33.710
And right at this
moment, the-- and I

00:33.710 --> 00:35.360
haven't drawn it
completely to scale--

00:35.360 --> 00:39.200
the owl is 15 feet
above the mouse.

00:39.200 --> 00:44.540
So this distance right
over here is 15 feet.

00:44.540 --> 00:48.840
And the mouse itself is 10
feet from the base of the lamp.

00:48.840 --> 00:49.640
Let me draw that.

00:49.640 --> 00:56.780
So the mouse is 10 feet
from the base of the lamp.

00:56.780 --> 00:58.850
And we also know-- we
have our little radar

00:58.850 --> 01:02.500
gun out-- we know that this
owl is diving straight down.

01:05.090 --> 01:09.090
And right now, it is
going 20 feet per second.

01:09.090 --> 01:17.290
So right now, this is going
down at 20 feet per second.

01:17.290 --> 01:20.210
Now what we're curious about
is we have the light over here.

01:20.210 --> 01:23.490
Light is coming from the
street lamp in every direction.

01:23.490 --> 01:26.080
And it creates a
shadow of the owl.

01:26.080 --> 01:28.130
So right now the
shadow is out here.

01:28.130 --> 01:30.440
And as the owl goes
further and further down,

01:30.440 --> 01:35.510
the shadow's going to move
to the left like that.

01:35.510 --> 01:38.040
And so, given everything that
we've set up right over here,

01:38.040 --> 01:44.300
the question is, at what
rate is the shadow moving?

01:44.300 --> 01:46.770
So let's think about what we
know and what we don't know.

01:46.770 --> 01:49.620
And to do that, let's
set up some variables.

01:49.620 --> 01:52.910
So let me draw the same thing a
little bit more geometrically.

01:52.910 --> 01:58.130
So let's say that this right
over here is the street light.

01:58.130 --> 02:00.350
That is 20 feet tall.

02:00.350 --> 02:06.770
And then this right over
here is the height of the owl

02:06.770 --> 02:09.780
right at this moment,
so this is 15 feet.

02:09.780 --> 02:11.520
The distance between
the base of the lamp

02:11.520 --> 02:14.650
and where the owl is going,
where that mouse is right now.

02:14.650 --> 02:16.760
This is 10 feet.

02:16.760 --> 02:18.880
And if I were to think
about where the shadow is,

02:18.880 --> 02:22.970
well, the light's
from right over here.

02:22.970 --> 02:25.790
And so the owl blocks the
light right over there,

02:25.790 --> 02:27.880
so the shadow is going
to be right over there.

02:27.880 --> 02:31.330
So if you just draw a straight
line from the source of light

02:31.330 --> 02:33.639
through the owl and
you just keep going

02:33.639 --> 02:35.180
and you hit the
ground, you are going

02:35.180 --> 02:36.596
to figure out where
the shadow is.

02:36.596 --> 02:44.500
So the shadow is going
to be right over here.

02:44.500 --> 02:46.110
It's going to be
right over there.

02:46.110 --> 02:49.140
And we need to figure out
how quickly is that moving.

02:49.140 --> 02:54.190
And it's going to be moving
in the leftward direction.

02:54.190 --> 02:57.110
So let's set up some
variables over here.

02:57.110 --> 02:59.090
So let's say-- so
what's changing?

02:59.090 --> 03:01.410
Well, we know that the height
of the owl is changing.

03:01.410 --> 03:03.000
So let's call that y.

03:03.000 --> 03:05.220
Right at this moment,
it's equal to 15,

03:05.220 --> 03:07.290
but it is actually changing.

03:07.290 --> 03:14.345
And let's call the
distance between the shadow

03:14.345 --> 03:19.790
and the mouse x.

03:19.790 --> 03:22.040
Now given this set
up, can we come up

03:22.040 --> 03:24.185
with a relationship
between x and y?

03:24.185 --> 03:26.310
And then using that
relationship, what we're really

03:26.310 --> 03:29.440
trying to come up with is,
what is the rate at which x

03:29.440 --> 03:31.780
is changing with
respect to time?

03:31.780 --> 03:33.880
We know what y is
right at this moment.

03:33.880 --> 03:37.800
We know what dy/dt is
right at this moment.

03:37.800 --> 03:39.790
Can we come up the
relationship between x and y

03:39.790 --> 03:41.540
and maybe take the
derivative with respect

03:41.540 --> 03:44.970
to t so we can
figure out what dx/dt

03:44.970 --> 03:48.990
is at a given moment in time?

03:48.990 --> 03:51.094
Well, both of these
triangles-- and when

03:51.094 --> 03:52.510
I say both of these
triangles, let

03:52.510 --> 03:54.720
me be clear what
I'm talking about.

03:54.720 --> 03:58.590
This triangle right over here,
the smaller triangle in green,

03:58.590 --> 04:01.600
is a similar triangle
to the larger triangle.

04:01.600 --> 04:04.630
This is a similar triangle
to this larger triangle

04:04.630 --> 04:06.640
that I am tracing in blue.

04:06.640 --> 04:08.770
It's similar to this larger one.

04:08.770 --> 04:10.250
How do I know that?

04:10.250 --> 04:13.430
Well, they both have a right
angle, right over here.

04:13.430 --> 04:15.190
They both share this angle.

04:15.190 --> 04:16.820
So all three-- they
have two angles

04:16.820 --> 04:22.280
in common-- then all three
angles must be in common.

04:22.280 --> 04:23.780
So they are similar
triangles, which

04:23.780 --> 04:26.390
means the ratio between
corresponding sides

04:26.390 --> 04:28.410
must be the same.

04:28.410 --> 04:33.790
So we know that
the ratio of x to y

04:33.790 --> 04:38.370
must be the ratio of this
entire base, which is x plus 10,

04:38.370 --> 04:42.860
to the height of the
larger triangle, to 20.

04:42.860 --> 04:45.280
And right there, we have a
relationship between x and y.

04:45.280 --> 04:48.150
And if we take the derivative
of both sides with respect to t,

04:48.150 --> 04:50.875
we're probably
doing pretty well.

04:50.875 --> 04:52.750
Now before taking the
derivative with respect

04:52.750 --> 04:54.660
to t-- I could do
it right over here--

04:54.660 --> 04:56.400
just to simplify
things a little bit,

04:56.400 --> 04:57.780
let me just cross multiply.

04:57.780 --> 05:01.030
So let me multiply both
sides of this equation by 20

05:01.030 --> 05:04.190
and y, just so that I
don't have as many things

05:04.190 --> 05:05.600
in the denominator.

05:05.600 --> 05:08.700
So on the left hand side
it simplifies to 20x.

05:08.700 --> 05:11.570
I don't want to write over it,
well, I'll just write-- 20x.

05:11.570 --> 05:15.580
And on the left
hand side is 20 x.

05:15.580 --> 05:17.200
And then on the
right hand side--

05:17.200 --> 05:26.254
let's see this cancels with
that-- we have xy plus 10y.

05:26.254 --> 05:27.670
And now let me
take the derivative

05:27.670 --> 05:30.990
of both sides with
respect to time.

05:30.990 --> 05:34.274
So the derivative of 20 times
something with respect to time

05:34.274 --> 05:36.940
is going to be the derivative of
20 times something with respect

05:36.940 --> 05:38.852
to the something,
which is just 20.

05:38.852 --> 05:40.560
That's the derivative
of 20x with respect

05:40.560 --> 05:45.030
to x, times dx or the derivative
of x with respect to t,

05:45.030 --> 05:46.669
is equal to.

05:46.669 --> 05:48.460
Now over here we're
going have to break out

05:48.460 --> 05:50.166
a little bit of
the product rule.

05:50.166 --> 05:52.040
So first we want to
figure out the derivative

05:52.040 --> 05:53.770
of x with respect to time.

05:56.900 --> 06:00.430
So the derivative of the first
thing times the second thing,

06:00.430 --> 06:01.590
times y.

06:01.590 --> 06:03.720
Plus just the first thing
times the derivative

06:03.720 --> 06:04.585
of the second thing.

06:04.585 --> 06:06.710
So derivative of y with
respect to t is just dy/dt.

06:09.690 --> 06:12.500
And then finally,
right over here.

06:12.500 --> 06:14.830
The derivative of
10y with respect to t

06:14.830 --> 06:18.320
is the derivative of 10y with
respect to y, which is just 10,

06:18.320 --> 06:21.247
times the derivative of y with
respect to t, which is dy/dt.

06:23.544 --> 06:24.460
And there you have it.

06:24.460 --> 06:29.400
You have your relationship
between dx/dt, dy/dt, and x

06:29.400 --> 06:30.689
and y.

06:30.689 --> 06:32.480
So let's just make sure
we have everything.

06:32.480 --> 06:35.682
This is what we're trying
to solve for, dx/dt.

06:35.682 --> 06:37.640
And let's see here, we
have another dx/dt here.

06:37.640 --> 06:39.300
We're going to try
to solve for that.

06:39.300 --> 06:42.380
We know what y is.
y is equal to 15.

06:42.380 --> 06:45.690
We know what dy/dt is, dy/dt.

06:45.690 --> 06:48.610
If we make the convention
since y is decreasing,

06:48.610 --> 06:50.340
we can say it's -20.

06:50.340 --> 06:52.840
So we know what this is.

06:52.840 --> 06:56.210
And so if we just know what,
so we know what this is.

06:56.210 --> 07:01.380
So if we just know what x
is we can solve for dx/dt.

07:01.380 --> 07:04.310
So what is x right
at this moment?

07:04.310 --> 07:06.505
Well, we can use
this first equation--

07:06.505 --> 07:08.130
we could actually
use this one up here,

07:08.130 --> 07:11.040
but this one is simplified a
little bit-- to actually solve

07:11.040 --> 07:11.680
for x.

07:11.680 --> 07:14.346
So let's do that, and then we'll
substitute back into this thing

07:14.346 --> 07:15.870
where we've taken
the derivative.

07:15.870 --> 07:22.400
So we get 20 times x
is equal to x times y.

07:22.400 --> 07:24.055
y is 15.

07:24.055 --> 07:26.180
And just remember, I could
have used this equation,

07:26.180 --> 07:27.970
but this is just
one step further.

07:27.970 --> 07:29.660
We've already
crossed multiplied.

07:29.660 --> 07:32.240
So it's x times y. y is 15.

07:32.240 --> 07:35.680
So it's x times 15,
plus 10 times y.

07:35.680 --> 07:39.047
Plus 10 times 15.

07:39.047 --> 07:39.880
Did I do that right?

07:39.880 --> 07:45.490
20x is equal to x times
15 plus 10 times 15.

07:45.490 --> 07:47.080
So let's see if you subtract.

07:47.080 --> 07:52.450
So just this is 20x is
equal to 15x, plus 150.

07:52.450 --> 07:55.020
Subtract 15x from
both sides, you

07:55.020 --> 07:59.900
get 5x is equal to 30--
sorry, 5x is equal to 150,

07:59.900 --> 08:01.420
my brain is getting ahead.

08:01.420 --> 08:03.330
5x is equal to 150.

08:03.330 --> 08:04.690
Divide both sides by 5.

08:04.690 --> 08:08.560
You get x is equal to 30 feet.

08:08.560 --> 08:11.760
x is equal to 30 feet
right at this moment.

08:11.760 --> 08:15.240
So this distance, just going
back to our original diagram.

08:15.240 --> 08:17.782
This distance right
over here is 30 feet.

08:17.782 --> 08:19.740
So let's substitute all
the values we know back

08:19.740 --> 08:23.580
into this equation to
actually solve for dx/dt.

08:23.580 --> 08:25.800
So we have-- so let me
do it right over here--

08:25.800 --> 08:29.860
we have 20 times dx/dt.

08:29.860 --> 08:31.830
I'll do that in orange,
we'll solve for that.

08:31.830 --> 08:33.288
Actually no, I
already used orange.

08:33.288 --> 08:36.260
So let's say dx/dt-- I'll
use this pink-- 20 times

08:36.260 --> 08:41.860
dx/dt is equal to dx/dt times y.

08:41.860 --> 08:44.400
y right now is 15 feet.

08:44.400 --> 08:50.070
So times 15, times-- I
didn't want to do that color.

08:50.070 --> 08:56.220
Times 15 plus x, we
already know that x is 30.

08:56.220 --> 08:59.160
Plus 30, times dy/dt.

08:59.160 --> 09:00.280
What is dy/dt?

09:00.280 --> 09:07.400
dy/dt, we could say is -20 feet
per second. y is decreasing,

09:07.400 --> 09:11.150
the bird is the bird is
diving down to get its dinner.

09:11.150 --> 09:16.530
So times negative-- well,
times 20 feet per second.

09:16.530 --> 09:18.090
So that's that right over there.

09:18.090 --> 09:20.640
Plus 10 times dy/dt.

09:20.640 --> 09:25.840
So plus 10 times
-20 feet per second.

09:25.840 --> 09:27.910
And now we just solve for dx/dt.

09:27.910 --> 09:29.160
So let's see, what do we have?

09:29.160 --> 09:34.280
We have 20 times-- let's see,
let me subtract 15 dx/dt from

09:34.280 --> 09:37.310
both sides of this equation--
and we get 5dx/dt's.

09:40.880 --> 09:44.040
I just subtracted this from
both sides of the equation.

09:44.040 --> 09:45.920
This is 15dx/dt this is 20.

09:45.920 --> 09:50.046
So that we have 5dx/dt's,
is equal to this

09:50.046 --> 09:55.250
is, this part right
over here is -600.

09:55.250 --> 09:59.120
And this part right
over here is -200.

09:59.120 --> 10:05.720
So it's equal to -800
feet per second-- or -800,

10:05.720 --> 10:08.350
and actually this will
actually be in feet per second.

10:08.350 --> 10:12.660
And so dx/dt is equal to,
dividing both sides by 5,

10:12.660 --> 10:15.720
5 times 16 is 80.

10:15.720 --> 10:20.640
So this is -160 feet per second.

10:20.640 --> 10:21.390
And we're done.

10:21.390 --> 10:25.760
And we see the shadow is
moving very, very, very, very

10:25.760 --> 10:26.780
fast to the left.

10:26.780 --> 10:28.869
x is decreasing,
and we see that.

10:28.869 --> 10:30.660
That's why we have this
negative sign here.

10:30.660 --> 10:32.300
The value of x is decreasing.

10:32.300 --> 10:38.140
It is moving to the left
at quite a nice speed here.
